# ==============================================================================
# ifupdown-extra defaults configuration
# ==============================================================================
# This file provides default settings for the ifupdown-extra network testing scripts.
# It is sourced by scripts located in /etc/network/if-{pre,}-up.d.
# The file is installed at /etc/default/network-test.
#
# Syntax: Variable=${Variable:-default_value}
# This shell syntax means: if the environment variable 'Variable' is set, use it;
# otherwise, use 'default_value' provided by this file.
# ==============================================================================

# DO_SYSLOG: Force logging of tests to syslog.
# Description: Set to 'yes' to force logging tests to syslog.
# Since standard output of the scripts is sent to the logging system,
# the default is 'no' to avoid duplicate logs. Setting to 'yes' forces
# direct logging.
DO_SYSLOG=${DO_SYSLOG:-{{ DO_SYSLOG | default('no') }}}

# ABORT_NO_LINK: Abort if network link is down.
# Description: Set to 'yes' to abort scripts if the network link is not up.
# This prevents configuration attempts on interfaces that are physically disconnected.
ABORT_NO_LINK=${ABORT_NO_LINK:-{{ ABORT_NO_LINK | default('no') }}}

# DO_CABLETEST: Run network cable/link negotiation tests.
# Description: Set to 'yes' to run network cable tests.
# Note: Depending on hardware and link negotiation speed, this test might
# falsely report "no cable connected" while the link is still negotiating.
# If this occurs on your system, set this to 'no'.
DO_CABLETEST=${DO_CABLETEST:-{{ DO_CABLETEST | default('yes') }}}

# DO_ARPING: Test for address conflicts via ARP.
# Description: Set to 'yes' to test if an IPv4 or IPv6 address is already assigned.
# The scripts use 'arping' for IPv4 and 'ndisc6' for IPv6 (if available).
# This helps detect network configuration errors (e.g., IP conflicts) before
# the interface is fully configured.
# Note: This introduces a delay during interface configuration as the scripts
# must wait for the probes to complete. Set to 'no' to skip this delay.
DO_ARPING=${DO_ARPING:-{{ DO_ARPING | default('yes') }}}

# ARP_COUNT: Number of ARP pings sent.
# Description: Number of ARP pings sent during ARP tests.
# Higher counts may help detect issues in overloaded networks but increase delay.
ARP_COUNT=${ARP_COUNT:-{{ ARP_COUNT | default(2) }}}

# ARP_TIMEOUT: Timeout for ARP tests.
# Description: Timeout for ARP tests in seconds.
# This value defines how long the script waits for an ARP reply.
# Note: This setting assumes the use of iputils-arping (/usr/bin/arping).
# If using the older 'arping' package, values are often expected in milliseconds
# (minimum 1500ms).
ARP_TIMEOUT=${ARP_TIMEOUT:-{{ ARP_TIMEOUT | default(3) }}}